  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nIt's another mostly clear morning area wide with temperatures \nranging from the 30s in parts of central MN to 40s and a few 50s\nacross southern MN and WI. A few think high clouds are streaming\nnortheast from the central Plains, but have been dissipating\nbefore reaching MN. Models are not handling the sky cover\nparticularly well and are too bullish with coverage. \n\nHighs yesterday exceeded the NBM 90th percentile by up to 7 \ndegrees in some locations. While today is expected to be cooler,\n925 mb temps suggest some locations will again exceed the 90th \npercentile. Highs in the mid to upper 60s are likely. This is \nexpected to continue to be the case through at least Thursday\nwith an Omega block in place and the mid level ridge axis\nanchored over the eastern Plains and Mississippi Valley. Highs \nthis period were trended closer to the 90th percentile, but this\nmay still be conservative given observations from Sunday and \nforecast temperature profiles.\n\nA deep trough off the coast of California midweek will make it\nto the Rockies Friday and spawn an area of low pressure over \nthe northern Rockies and southern Canada. Meridional flow will\nsteer the system north into Canada with its associated cold \nfront making slow eastward progress this weekend. Chances of\nshowers return as the front approaches, but there remains large\nspread in the guidance with timing and how far east the front\nwill make it before potentially washing out.",
